      "headline": "Big Mo and the Merry-Go-Round",
      "keyInsight": "Getting a massive metal merry-go-round to spin is pure agony at first. You push with all your strength and it barely creeps forward an inch. You groan, sweat, and wonder why you even bothered. But once that heavy wheel gets moving, physics takes over. Suddenly, a gentle tap keeps it spinning at breakneck speed. That invisible force is momentum, or 'Big Mo'. Most people quit while pushing the heavy wheel on day three. If you stay consistent, the universe eventually pushes the wheel for you.",
      "content": "Think about a giant steam locomotive sitting cold on the tracks. If you place a tiny one-inch wooden wedge under the front wheel, that mighty engine cannot budge. It sits trapped by a sliver of pine. But fire up that furnace and get the train barreling down the rails at sixty miles per hour. Now, that same train can smash straight through a solid concrete barrier five feet thick without slowing down. That is the sheer magic of momentum.\n\nMost people live in a state of stop-and-go traffic with their habits. They start an exercise routine in January, push hard for two weeks, and then take a break. The moment you hit the brakes, your train stops dead on the tracks. Restarting from a dead stop costs eighty percent of your fuel and will. When you stop, you have to fight the heavy wooden wedge all over again.\n\nBig Mo loves simple routines. When you lock in a morning rhythm and an evening wind-down, your brain shifts to autopilot. You stop wasting willpower on daily negotiations with yourself. Brush teeth, drink water, lace shoes, open notebook. The heavy flywheel starts turning on its own.\n\nKeep your wheels rolling even when you feel tired. It is far easier to jog two miles than to stop for a month and restart. Protect your rhythm at all costs. Once Big Mo climbs aboard your train, success stops feeling like a grueling chore and starts feeling like an unstoppable slide downhill.",
